{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he shall take the two goats, etc.</b> This passage requires explanation, for why would HaShem command such a thing? And if it is among the commandments for which HaShem gave no reason, it should have said ״חֻקָּה״ (a statute). And the difficulty grows even greater according to the words of the masters of blessed memory (Ra'aya Mehemna on Aḥarei; Zohar vol. III, 101a), that Azazel is an aspect of the side of evil — behold, it is nearly, Heaven forbid, as though the matter resembles idolatrous worship! And all the more so that HaShem commands (Yoma 67a) to make the goat for HaShem's Name equal to this goat that belongs to something else — the matter is astounding!" "<b>Let us see</b> to say, according to the premises that our Rabbis of blessed memory informed us — and this comes first: We learned in Mishnat Ḥasidim (Avot, ch. 4): One who performs one mitzvah acquires for himself one advocate, and one who commits one transgression acquires for himself one accuser — end of quote. And the matter is explained well according to the words of the holy Zohar (Zohar vol. II, 76a; vol. III, 122a and what follows), and with supernal reasoning according to the Arizal of blessed memory who enters the halls of the King — for from the transgression itself an evil thing is born, which is the very sin that was committed, and it is delivered into the hand of its doer, as they said: ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״ (Jeremiah 2:19) — your evil shall chastise you; ״וַתְּמוּגֵנוּ בְּיַד עֲוֹנֵינוּ״ (Isaiah 64:6) — and we melted away in the hand of our iniquities. Therefore, when HaShem forgave David, He informed him that the evil force born from the improper deed had been lost/destroyed, as it says (II Samuel 12:13): ״גַּם ה׳ הֶעֱבִיר חַטָּאתְךָ לֹא תָמוּת״ — HaShem has also removed your sin; you shall not die. The intent of this is: since the sin that a person commits creates from itself what is exacted from its doer, it follows that when the destructive force is lost, there is nothing destructive. And this is what HaShem said to him — that after He has removed his sin he will not die, for there is nothing to cause death. From now on: since through deeds evil things are created that harm and afflict a person, HaShem, whose goodness is preferred upon us, commanded that anyone who commits an improper deed unwittingly, from among a person's unwitting sins, shall bring a sacrifice, and He commanded that the owner of the sacrifice lean his hands upon it. And the reason is that through this those external forces that were generated from his evil deeds are drawn out upon that sacrifice. And this action, HaShem informed us, will be accomplished through the special property of a person bringing his sacrifice to the house of our Elohim and leaning upon it there — the matter will be done with the complete power of the Elohim of spirits for all flesh. And this is the reason HaShem commanded the act of leaning hands (semikhah) for sin-offerings. Now, every act performed with a sin-offering — slaughtering and burning — activates that aspect drawn from the person into that sacrifice by means of his leaning, as mentioned; and through this the aspect of evil is uprooted. And you already know that a sin-offering (חַטָּאת) comes only for sins committed unwittingly (shogeg), because its impurity (zuhamah) is not abundant, whereas for a sin (avon) that a person commits intentionally (mezid), whose impurity is abundant, no sacrifice is brought. And this is what it says (Proverbs 21:27): ״זֶבַח רְשָׁעִים תּוֹעֵבָה״ — the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination, for HaShem blessed be He abhors bringing such an abomination upon His altar — for there is nothing more repulsive in the world than the repulsiveness and contamination of sin; may the Merciful One save us." "<b>According to</b> this premise the matter will be explained correctly, with the preface of what our Rabbis of blessed memory wrote in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״וְכִפֶּר עַל הַקֹּדֶשׁ מִטֻּמְאֹת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמִפִּשְׁעֵיהֶם״ etc. — for intentional defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, the goat offered inside [the Sanctuary] and Yom Kippur effect atonement; and for all other transgressions in the Torah, the minor and the severe, the intentional and the unwitting, known and unknown, positive and negative commandments, karet and death-penalties of the court — the goat sent away effects atonement — end of their language. And in the Talmud, this is their language: And the goat of Azazel bears upon it all the intentional sins, minor and severe, and unwitting ones, etc. And the priests too, their atonement is through the sent-away goat, as they said of blessed memory in tractate Zevaḥim chapter 1 (see Menahot 92a; Shevu'ot 13a), and this is their language: Rabbi Yehudah says — the priests too achieve atonement through the sent-away goat. Now that HaShem has informed us of all this, behold — here is a sufficient reason before you: the two goats bear all the contamination of the soul's afflictions. The goat that bears upon it intentional defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things — HaShem accepted it and did not abhor it. But the goat bearing the contaminations of all transgressions, minor and severe, HaShem commanded that it be destroyed and consumed outside the sacred precinct. And He commanded that Aaron lean his hands upon it, for it is the emissary of Israel, and everything done there [with it] was as though the person's emissary himself did it — for a person's emissary is like him. And it says ״וְנָתַן אוֹתָם עַל רֹאשׁ הַשָּׂעִיר הַחַי״ — the verse explains the matter well, that through the leaning he is placing all those contaminating impurities upon its head. And it says ״וְנָשָׂא הַשָּׂעִיר״ — it informs that there is found in it an aspect capable of receiving and bearing that evil unto a land, etc." "<b>And to understand</b> the matter — a parable: to a king who examined the garments of his sons and saw upon them clean garments with a spot upon them; he would rub it off with his hand and give the garment to his son. But when he saw a garment that was very soiled, he would give it to a certain servant of his servants, one who would not be repulsed by the repulsive thing. So too is this matter: the King is our King, may His Name be exalted. When a sacrifice is brought before Him, if it is a detail and the purity of a spot of any size — which are like the spots of unwitting error, or one particular detail of defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things as mentioned — the sacrifice will not be abhorrent to Him. But all the contamination of all the transgressions that Israel committed, minor and severe, is a great repulsiveness, and He said to take it to a certain servant, from among those most distant from the kingship, in that he is among the lowest and lightest in worth, and he shall smash and destroy and obliterate them. And this is nothing but giving him a despised service to perform. Even so, it is an elevation for him that the Master considered him to do something from what had been before El in the Temple, and that goat is made equivalent with the goat offered before El. And the reason HaShem commanded doing so and did not say to take a goat from the market and send it to Azazel after confessing over it, is that it is impossible for all the impurities that Israel committed through their evil deeds to gather and come upon the head of the goat except by bringing the goat before HaShem. And after it has been brought before HaShem, from then on it is established that this is the goat that shall bear all the abomination and go off to the land of Gezerah. Therefore He commanded to cast lots, and He who chooses shall select the goat that He shall choose in His wisdom, blessed be He, from among living creatures — all the more so according to what the kabbalists said, that there are many souls and sparks within living creatures, even non-speaking ones, and who knows but He, blessed be His Name. Also there is a sod in the lottery (Zohar vol. III, 101a) — and understand:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:58.811595802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}